Z||000000||engine18||04-24-2000||06:57 PM||||On Saturday, I attempted to start my 85 V6, but the engine did not turn over, no engine console lights came on.. I Remove the battery, had to purchase a new (Battery Bad).  Installed new battery, the car started with no problems... On Monday, The engine would not turn over, no console/engine lights... It's not making a noise... Jumped started the vehicle... Engine started with everything working properly.. Is it the Alternator or starter...  Can removing the alternator be a one-man job.||||reg||9
Z||000001||theogre||04-24-2000||07:06 PM||||most places will check the alternator for free. many can do it on or off the car.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DiggityDan||04-24-2000||07:07 PM||||Sounds like you have a grounding problem, could be alternator, probably not unless its shorted inside. Something has grounded itself out causing the battery to run down while it sits. Could really be anything.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Capt Fiero||04-25-2000||01:38 PM||||This may sound dumb, but I too had this problem and it turned out to be the trunk light was not turning off. I would close the trunk like everyone does and just assume that the light was off, kinda like a fridge door. But you never know.||||reg||1
Z||000004||general||04-27-2000||12:32 AM||||   I agree with theogre.Get the alternator checked at a local parts store. If it's bad,the best way to remove it seems to be to take it out through the bottom.It only has a couple of wires and about 2 bolts.  ||||reg||3
Z||000005||Ozzy||04-27-2000||01:53 AM||||I just replaced the alternator on my 87 GT. It was pretty easy do drop the alternator from underneath but I have a manual tranny too. I read some posts from guys who have autos and said this was not as easy. Had to move some stuff outta the way and take out from the top.<P>I agree with above. Maybe something draining or battery not getting charged. If you have a Volt/ohm meter with a DC amps option, remove your POS+ battery lead (with a charged battery) and connect your meter in series between the POS+ cable and POS+ post of the batt. Read the current draw. Should only be very low current. (0.125ADC or less) Do this with trunk closed. (Deactivating trunk light) If higher, open trunk and disconnect trunk light and recheck amperage. If lower, trunk light is not turning off when trunk is closed.<P>Ozzy<p>[This message has been edited by Ozzy (edited 04-27-2000).]||||reg||1
